<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">40963314</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>18</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>18</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">1660-9379</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>21</Volume><Issue>931</Issue><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Sep</Month><Day>17</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Revue medicale suisse</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Rev Med Suisse</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>[Bradyarrhythmia and topical timolol : highlighting the need for medication reconciliation].</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1670</StartPage><EndPage>1671</EndPage><MedlinePgn>1670-1671</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.53738/REVMED.2025.21.931.47723</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Ophthalmic beta-blockers such as timolol can exert clinically significant systemic effects due to high transnasal and conjunctival absorption. We describe a 94-year-old woman who developed symptomatic bradyarrhythmia, including sinus pauses and atrial fibrillation with slow ventricular response, attributed to topical timolol. Symptoms resolved following discontinuation and substitution with non-beta-blocker therapy. This case underscores the importance of comprehensive medication reconciliation, explicitly addressing non-oral routes of administration. In elderly, polymorbid patients, unrecognized topical medications may lead to delayed diagnosis and preventable adverse events. Clinicians should systematically assess all formulations when investigating potential drug-related cardiovascular effects.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Prodolliet</LastName><ForeName>Aristide</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Service des urgences, H&#xf4;pital de La Tour, 1217 Meyrin/Gen&#xe8;ve.<